---
import FeaturePage from '../layouts/FeaturePage.astro';
import FeatureActions from '../components/FeatureActions.astro';
import Video from '../components/Video.astro';
import ProductMedia from '../components/ProductMedia.astro';
import trace from '../assets/tracing.webp';
import poster from '../assets/tracing-end.webp';
---

<FeaturePage
  title="Purr — HTTP Client with Jaeger Tracing"
  description="Send a request, inspect the response, and open the correlated Jaeger trace without leaving your API client."
  name="Jaeger tracing"
  variant="trace-page"
>
  <header class="feature-hero container trace-hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">HTTP CLIENT / JAEGER TRACING</p>
    <h1>
      Follow the request
      <br />
      <span class="muted">past the response.</span>
    </h1>
    <p class="feature-lead">
      Send the request, enable tracing, and Purr correlates the response with the matching trace so
      you can open it directly from the request you just ran.
    </p>
    <FeatureActions docs="/docs/tracing/" label="Read tracing docs" />
  </header>
  <div class="container trace-stage">
    <ol class="feature-flow" aria-label="Request to trace workflow">
      <li>Send request</li>
      <li>Receive response</li>
      <li>Correlate trace</li>
      <li>Inspect spans</li>
    </ol>
    <Video
      name="tracing"
      poster={poster}
      label="Follow the response into its Jaeger trace and inspect a selected span."
    />
  </div>
  <section class="container feature-editorial">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">WHY I ADDED THIS</p>
      <h2>Debugging shouldn’t start with searching for the request you just sent.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="feature-prose">
      <p>
        When I was debugging slower endpoints, sending the request was usually the easy part. The
        annoying part started after that.
      </p>
      <p>
        I would send a request with curl, get the response, then open Jaeger and try to find the
        trace that belonged to that exact request. If the endpoint touched several services or did
        some CPU-heavy work, I would repeat this over and over while trying to understand where the
        time actually went.
      </p>
      <p>A slow response tells you that something took too long. It rarely tells you what.</p>
      <ul class="diagnostic-questions">
        <li>Was it the database?</li>
        <li>Was it another service?</li>
        <li>Was it CPU work inside the application?</li>
        <li>Did one downstream request dominate the whole response time?</li>
        <li>Did my fix actually improve the part I thought it did?</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The information already existed in tracing. The frustrating part was jumping from the API
        client to another tool and then searching for the trace manually. That felt like something
        the client could do for me.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container feature-band trace-context">
    <p class="eyebrow">REQUEST AND TRACE STAY TOGETHER</p>
    <div class="feature-editorial inner">
      <h2>
        One request,
        <br />
        <span class="muted">one debugging context.</span>
      </h2>
      <div class="feature-prose">
        <p>Tracing in Purr is attached to the request workflow.</p>
        <p>
          You send a request as usual. If tracing is enabled for it, Purr looks for the trace
          context associated with that execution and makes the matching trace available directly
          from the response.
        </p>
        <p>
          You don’t need to copy IDs between tools or remember which request happened at which
          timestamp.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container feature-section-block">
    <div class="feature-heading-row">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">SEE WHERE THE TIME WENT</p>
        <h2>
          The response time
          <br />
          <span class="muted">is only the beginning.</span>
        </h2>
      </div>
      <p>
        Once the trace is loaded, inspect the service tree and span durations to understand how the
        request moved through the system.
      </p>
    </div>
    <ProductMedia
      src={trace}
      alt="Purr Jaeger trace showing a 200 OK response, trace identifier, service waterfall and the selected request handler span with its duration"
      caption="The real request, its correlated trace, and the selected span in one view."
    />
    <div class="trace-example">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">ILLUSTRATIVE TIMING EXAMPLE</p>
        <h3>824 ms tells only part of the story.</h3>
        <p>That is much more useful than knowing only that the endpoint was “slow”.</p>
      </div>
      <ol aria-label="Illustrative nested span durations, not additive timings">
        {[
          ['API gateway', 824],
          ['Request handler', 790],
          ['Orders service', 650],
          ['Inventory request', 520],
          ['Database query', 470],
        ].map(([name, time]) => (
          <li>
            <span>{name}</span>
            <span class="span-track" aria-hidden="true">
              <i style={`width:${(Number(time) / 824) * 100}%`}></i>
            </span>
            <code>{time} ms</code>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container trace-debug-loop">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">USEFUL WHILE FIXING</p>
      <h2>I wanted this for the request I am actively testing.</h2>
    </div>
    <ol class="feature-flow" aria-label="Debugging iteration">
      <li>Change the code</li>
      <li>Send the request</li>
      <li>Inspect the trace</li>
      <li>Compare the result</li>
    </ol>
    <div class="feature-prose">
      <p>
        Monitoring tools are great when an alert fires in production. But a lot of debugging happens
        before that.
      </p>
      <p>
        You change the code, send the same request again, compare the behavior, and check whether
        the bottleneck moved or disappeared.
      </p>
      <p>
        That loop is where having the trace directly beside the request becomes useful. Purr is
        meant to make that loop shorter.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container feature-pair">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">CURRENT SUPPORT</p>
      <h2>
        Jaeger today.
        <br />
        <span class="muted">More providers later.</span>
      </h2>
      <p>
        The current tracing integration works with Jaeger. Configure your Jaeger connection, enable
        tracing for a request, and Purr handles the request-to-trace workflow from there.
      </p>
      <p>
        The integration layer is being designed so other tracing and observability providers can be
        added later without changing how the request workflow works.
      </p>
      <a class="text-link" href="/docs/tracing/">
        Set up Jaeger →
      </a>
    </div>
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">KEEP IT OPTIONAL</p>
      <h2>
        No tracing
        <br />
        <span class="muted">setup required.</span>
      </h2>
      <p>
        Tracing is not required to use Purr. If all you need is an HTTP or{' '}
        <a href="/graphql-client/">GraphQL client</a>, nothing changes.
      </p>
      <p>
        Tracing only becomes part of the workflow when you configure it and enable it for the
        requests where it is useful.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container feature-end">
    <h2>
      Send the request.
      <br />
      <span class="muted">Then see what actually happened.</span>
    </h2>
    <p>
      Purr keeps the response and the trace connected so debugging does not start with searching
      through another tool.
    </p>
    <FeatureActions docs="/docs/tracing/" label="Tracing documentation" />
  </section>
</FeaturePage>
